2026-06-13 chore/pin-requirements — CPO scope ruling on #425 (audit F20). F20's text said "pin requirements.txt deps" and noted bs4 "appears unused" + functions-framework "likely belongs in ingestion". During investigation the builder confirmed bs4 AND pandas have zero usage anywhere (no import, no DataFrame/load_table_from_dataframe), requests is used, and functions-framework is used only in ingestion (already declared in ingestion/api_football/requirements.txt). The builder surfaced this to the CPO with two questions:
  (1) Scope of #425 — pin-only / pin + drop bs4 only / pin + remove all dead.
      CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-06-13): "Pin + remove dead" — pin the used dep (requests==2.33.1), DROP the two unused deps (beautifulsoup4 AND pandas) and the redundant root functions-framework, leave pyarrow + the floor-pinned deps untouched (out of F20 scope). The pandas removal — NOT named in F20's text — was explicitly stated in the question context ("beautifulsoup4 + pandas have zero usage anywhere") and in the chosen option's description ("DROP unused (beautifulsoup4, pandas)"); the CPO approved it knowingly.
  (2) PR structure for #425 + #427.
      CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-06-13): two separate PRs (#425 deps, #427 data-contract docs) — independent, different reviewer routings, independent merge.
  Recorded here so the contract's decisions_taken cites a locatable ruling (scope-auditor finding, iteration-1 FAIL).

2026-06-13 feat/dim-player-team-season — CPO ruling on object classification (PR A of the player-model redesign). The iteration-2 scope-auditor FAILed: per layering.md's dimension-qualification rules, dim_player_team_season does not earn dim_ status — it is a conformed many-to-many RELATIONSHIP (player↔team↔season rostered membership), not an entity, and it is attribute-less (the "degenerate dimension" pattern the rules exclude). layering.md recognizes only dim_ (entities) and fct_ (events); no category exists for a relationship/mapping table, and there is no precedent in 3_core. CPO design-session findings (2026-06-13): the conformed reusable mapping is a CORE concern (layering.md 3_core owns "relationship logic... that marts/intermediate reuse rather than re-derive"); base cannot host it (base may not be the system of record and cannot ref the core dim_competition_season needed to resolve season_sk).
  CPO RULING (2026-06-13): the model name should reflect WHERE it sits (core), so KEEP the dim_ prefix AND add a _mapping suffix → final name `dim_player_team_season_mapping` (dim_ = core home; _mapping = it is a relationship table, not an entity). ADJUST layering.md's rules to recognize a conformed relationship/mapping table as a valid dim_ — exempt from the Entity condition and the degenerate-dimension exclusion, still required to satisfy Reuse + Conformance, declare its relationship grain, and test grain uniqueness; grain is one row per association occurrence (many rows per participating entity). Add layering.md to the task scope (amendment) + inventory row. Exact rule wording to be confirmed before the gated commit.

2026-06-13 chore/retire-transfers — CPO ruling to RETIRE the transfers chain entirely. Builder investigation (this session): transfers are not consumed anywhere — fct_transfer is a leaf with zero mart/export/UI readers; the only internal use was a dim_player identity fallback feeding that unconsumed fact; nothing in scripts/ or site/ references transfers. Club changes are derivable from dim_player_team_season_mapping (season-level roster diffs) if ever needed. The builder asked whether transfers carry analytical value or are nice-to-know, and whether to drop the identity source only vs retire the whole chain.
  CPO RULING (2026-06-13, conversation): "I tend to option B. Transfers is not needed, so remove it entirely." → retire end-to-end. This carries the COST approval to stop the daily /transfers ingest (cost-non-negotiable rule); and on the follow-up "BQ table dropped too?", CPO ANSWER: "yes" → explicit DESTRUCTIVE authorization to drop the RAW_APIF_TRANSFERS BigQuery table (post-merge, so the 04:00 UTC run cannot recreate it). Follows the RAW_APIF_ROUNDS retirement precedent. Recorded here so the contract's decisions_taken cites a locatable ruling (iteration-2 scope-auditor §10 finding).

2026-06-13 governance/gate-integrity-409 — CPO GATE-LIFT + fix-mechanism picks for the #409 (F10/F11) + #421 (F12) gate-integrity bundle. The issue reserves the fix MECHANISM to the CPO at task time. Builder presented the options; CPO decided (conversation + AskUserQuestion, 2026-06-13):
  GATE-LIFT: "Do it" — explicit authorization to edit the protected gate machinery (.claude/review_routing.json, .claude/hooks/git_discipline.py, scripts/check_task_artifacts.py) for this bundle.
  F10 (contract-only commit reaches main unreviewed): CPO ANSWER (a) — stop exempting contract.md from the artifact_only lane, so any commit containing it requires scope-auditor review.
  F11 (CI can't bind review.md to the PR diff): CPO ANSWER (a) — make the review hash cover the diff EXCLUDING the bookkeeping artifacts (contract.md + code ARE hashed); CI recomputes git diff base...HEAD over the same exclusion and compares. Binds review to the PR code+contract. Consequence (accepted): contract.md must be final in the reviewed commit — no post-commit contract amendments.
  F12 (#421): demote the redundant global ESCALATE/ANSWER count to a commented secondary backstop; per-section loop authoritative.
